{
 "result": "fibmod.py is built and verified: it prints fib(n) mod 1_000_000_007 for n up to 10**18, and the benchmark runs at about 2.2M calls/s against a registered baseline of 92,368 (roughly 24x). The CLI path uses plain fast doubling after reducing n mod (M+1), using F(n+M+1) \u2261 \u2212F(n) mod M. The importable `fib()` adds three lazily built 1024-entry tables, so each call is two table combinations instead of a 30-step loop. Evidence: check.py passes (fib(0..5000) against naive iteration, 2000 random n up to 10**18 against an independent matrix-power reference, and the CLI for 10 \u2192 55 and 10**18 \u2192 209783453); a further cross-check of both functions on 20,000 random n plus edge cases (M, M+1, 2M+1, 2M+2, M\u22121) ran clean. The speed-up is in the in-process `fib()` that bench.py times; a single CLI run takes about 30 ms, of which about 27.5 ms is Python startup, so the command-line run itself is not noticeably faster. No `try` daughters were used; the gains came from direct edits.",
